CBOOT: the bootstrap C bundle of patsopt
========================================

The compiler is written in ATS2. To build it without any ATS compiler, the C code that a working patsopt
generates for every module is distributed as a bundle in this directory. The bundle is tracked (a fresh clone builds
with `make bootstrap && make` at the root): the generated files are marked in ../../.gitattributes (no diffs); only the build
products (obj_*/, patsopt, libatsopt.a) are ignored. It is path-independent: see "Path independence" below.

Generating (needs a working patsopt and python3; see ../README)

    make -C src CBOOT [PATSOPT=...]       # runs mkboot.sh

mkboot.sh generates, for C3NSTRINTKND=gmpknd and intknd, the C code of every module (pats_*_dats.c, pats_error_sats.c, the
main module with and without main() for libatsopt, libatsopt_ext_dats.c) and writes:

    *.c               the C code that does not depend on the integer kind (the time stamps and all the comments of the generated code are removed by stripc.py, the paths are made neutral)
    gmpknd/*.c intknd/*.c   the modules that differ (pats_intinf, pats_lintprgm_myint, pats_constraint3_solve)
    pats_runtime_ats1.c, libatsopt_ext.c   hand-written C of src/
    inc/              the headers that the C code includes: ccomp/runtime, prelude/CATS, libats/CATS, src/*.cats
    modules.mk        the lists of the files (included by Makefile)

The generated *_gen*.hats files (arena encodings) are already expanded in the C code: python3 is not needed to use the bundle.

Path independence

The generated C holds the absolute path of the tree and of the stage-0 compiler: in strings (location of assertions and
exceptions: "/path/src/pats_error.sats:FatalErrorExn") and in identifiers (patsopt mangles every character that is not
[A-Za-z0-9_] into _%03o_: "/path/src/pats_error.sats__FatalErrorExn" is _057_path_057_src_057_pats_error_056_sats__FatalErrorExn).
stripc.py --root replaces each root (the PATSHOME of the build, and the PATSHOME of the stage-0 compiler when that is another
install) by the word PATSHOME in both forms, in every file, so that the names stay consistent across the modules and
the bundle generated in any directory is byte-identical (the strings are only diagnostics: no file is opened through them).

Using (a C compiler, make and libgmp; nothing else)

    make bootstrap                         # (root of the repository) patsopt from the bundle, then the whole build with it: bin/patsopt, bin/patscc, ..
                                           #   KIND=intknd for no gmp; CC=, CFLAGS= are honoured
    make -C src/CBOOT -j8                  # ./patsopt (gmp if <gmp.h> is there, else the machine integers: KIND is probed)
    make -C src/CBOOT KIND=intknd          # ./patsopt without gmp
    make -C src/CBOOT libatsopt.a          # the library version (the API of utils/libatsopt)
    make -C src/CBOOT CC=clang CFLAGS=-O1 LDFLAGS=...

Then build the real compiler from the sources with it (stage 2; the bundle's patsopt is stage 1, compiled from the C that
the compiler that generated the bundle produced):

    make -C src                                        # finds CBOOT/patsopt (after PATSOPT= and bin/patsopt, before the patsopt of the PATH)
    make -C src PATSOPT=$PWD/src/CBOOT/patsopt         # PATSHOME = this repository
    # or: make -f Makefile_devl PATSOPT=$PWD/src/CBOOT/patsopt all

If the compiler that generated the bundle is itself a stage-2 build, the C of the bundle is the C that the normal build
compiles: the patsopt of the bundle and the one of `make -C src` are then the same program (up to the path strings,
which the bundle holds as PATSHOME/src/..).
